  "content": {
    "id": "p15-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "Find the least number of times that a regular die must be rolled to be at least 90% certain that you’ll obtain at least one six.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$13$ rolls.\n\nUse the probability of no sixes, $(\\frac56)^n$, and compare $n=12$ and $n=13$.",
      "worked": "Let $n$ be the number of independent rolls. The probability of not rolling a six on each roll is $\\frac56$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\Pr(\\text{at least one six})&=1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n\\\\1-\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&\\ge\\frac9{10}\\\\\\left(\\frac56\\right)^n&\\le\\frac1{10}\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}\\left(\\frac56\\right)^{12}&=\\frac{244140625}{2176782336}>\\frac1{10}\\\\\\left(\\frac56\\right)^{13}&=\\frac{1220703125}{13060694016}<\\frac1{10}\\end{align*}$$\nThe probability of no sixes decreases as $n$ increases, so the least number is $13$ rolls.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 82
  },
